<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mount Lykaion</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Mount Lycaeus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mount Lykaion is a mountain in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arcadia">Arcadia</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a>, rising to about 1,400 metres. Its summit held an ash altar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a> Lykaios, the site of ancient sacrifices and athletic games, and local legend made it the birthplace of Zeus. In 227 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleomenes-iii">Cleomenes III</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> defeated the Achaean League army near the mountain.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mount-lykaion.jpg" alt="Mount Lykaion" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Danno1 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lykaion_looking_East.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
